[Music]  hey everyone its Peter Brown standing in  for freelancer Daniel Starkey for his  review of Dark Souls 3 the ring city the  cobblestone pathways and towering  cathedrals of the eponymous ring city  twist and melt into one another as they  fall into oblivion here at the edge of  the world and old woman quips that she  feels like a God looking over all  creation as it races towards the end of  everything these twisted landscapes make  for a surreal backdrop and a fitting  metaphor for the final chapter of Dark  Souls it's fortunate then that the Ring  City doesn't waste any of that creative  energy as weird as the world of Dark  Souls can be the brutality tends to keep  them grounded and tangible with the ring  city however these pretenses fall away  your introduction shunts you through a  series of monster packed corridors  interspersed with steep bizarrely  non-lethal drops down cliffs these show  your gradual descent into the  nightmarish plane of the ring city an  apparent mosaic of the settings of the  series other entries now caked in ash  and dust and balance moments of  desperation against bursts of fatalism  it's striking tense and emblematic of  the expansion as a whole the ring city  takes all of Dark Souls ideas and  presses them to the extreme in previous  games high-risk high-reward play was  advantageous but here it's essential you  won't find your first bonfire a shelter  from the universe's grotesque monsters  for quite some time and even then it  doesn't seem too useful you'll rush out  and die without a chance to learn much  angels guard much of the area and they  can spot you from some ways off the  subsequent salvo of golden light Spears  can tear through your health in an  instant if you're not careful and these  angels can't be killed directly at first  that seems like a breach of the series  guiding mantra of tough but fair  however it's there to upend expectations  and encourage new styles of play in this  case stealth which the series had yet to  explore once you know where and how to  move the area is still a challenge but  in true Souls fashion it's far from  insurmountable  it's a shame then that the ring cities  brisk pace just isn't enough to give  these new ideas the attention they  deserve just as each new concept starts  coming into view you move on an  undercurrent of weighty themes in the  dense overworld more than compensate  however as I send off for this trilogy  the Ring City has to tie a lot of  disparate threads together and it  manages this by focusing on its  characters figures like patches a  deceitful murderous thief conclude their  arcs here  people in the Dark Souls universe fade  as their will does over time they lose  their minds and go hollow throughout the  series this little bit of lore has been  leveraged as an analog for the player if  the game gets too hard and you give up  your avatar could be thought of as going  hollow within the game world it's  telling them that on the precipice of  the apocalypse the folks who made it are  dogged albeit with banal drives these  games have always used conviction of  purpose as an analog for how a weak  willed person can persist in spite of  suffering patches while he lacks  compassion and strength of character  lives on through greed and suffering  tortured though his soul may be  unearthing what sorts of karma this  world holds for cheats becomes one of a  series of lenses the ring city holds up  for you as it asks you to look back on  your time with these characters and  stories and reflect on what they've  meant to you oh we are head square on  your shorter is it I thought that  clamoring tin can  lost about hey here we go again anyone  less interested in dissecting what it  all means we'll have plenty to chew on  as well the Ring City has a total of  four unique bosses more than any other  such add-on for any game in the series  and confronting each is a blast nothing  here is half done and it shows as with  the rest of the expansion bosses here  remix classic ideas and incorporate a  bevy of new twists such as inviting  players to fight alongside a boss in  against you another series first the  last duel is a poetic conclusion pitting  you against a force tied to the fate of  the world and one of the series biggest  mysteries the story of the furtive pygmy  the fight is raw and chaotic with your  enemy mirroring the patterns and strikes  of Artorias a warrior corrupted by  darkness and the central figure of the  five-year-old artorias of the abyss  expansion exactly who the final boss of  the series is I'll leave out but it does  provide a perfect narrative arc for the  franchise to end on C is like that R  where Dark Souls succeeds best it's not  just inventive level design or epic boss  battles or even an engaging story  it's the merging of them all and that's  made even more memorable when the  choreography of a battle gives slight  nods to its inspiration make no mistake  though while the ring city much like  Dark Souls 3 as a whole leans a bit on  nostalgia and illusions it earns in a  steam space along its forebears  the ring city moves and ends a little  too quickly well before it gets all the  mileage you can out of its settling and  premises but that's a small knock  against what's otherwise an exceptional  send-off a well of inspired locales and  a renewed focus on the series obsession  with cycles death and rebirth in the  passage of time plus a few spectacularly  designed and animated foes round out a  worthy conclusion to a storied franchise  [Music]  you

